.TH cp 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
cp, mv, ln \- copy, move and link files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp
.I "source dest"
.br
.B cp
.I "source ... directory"
.br
.B mv
.I "source dest"
.br
.B ln
.I "source dest"
.SH DESCRIPTION
One binary provides all three commands; it looks at the name it was
called by to decide what to do.
.PP
.B cp
copies
.I source
to
.IR dest ,
or copies each
.I source
into
.I directory
under its own name.
.PP
.B mv
does the same and then removes the original.
.PP
.B ln
makes
.I dest
a second directory entry for the same file.  Both names then refer to
one inode: changing the file through one name changes it under the
other, and the file survives until the last name is removed.
.SH OPTIONS
None.  The POSIX options
.RB ( \-i ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-R )
are not implemented.
.SH NOTES
There are no symbolic links in FUZIX, so
.B ln
has no
.B \-s
and cannot make one.  A hard link cannot span filesystems and cannot
point at a directory.
.PP
.B mv
copies and then unlinks even when both names are on the same
filesystem, where
.BR rename (2)
would be instant.  Moving a large file is as slow as copying it, and
needs room for both copies while it runs.
.PP
Neither
.B cp
nor
.B mv
copies a directory tree.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR mkdir (1)
